         pros: ['Official USGA handicap tracking at no cost', 'GPS yardage on 40,000+ courses without a subscription', 'Clean interface — no bloated features for recreational golfers'],
         cons: ['GPS less precise than dedicated watch units', 'Shot tracking manual — no automatic logging'], price: 'Free / $8/month premium' },
-      { h2: 'Best All-in-One: 18Birdies', rating: 4.5, affiliateKey: '18birdies', badge: 'BEST ALL-IN-ONE', body: "18Birdies does more things well than any other golf app. GPS, scoring, handicap, stat tracking, virtual caddie, and a rangefinder mode — all in one interface that is genuinely pleasant to use mid-round. The home screen shows your next round forecast, recent scores, and handicap trend without digging through menus.\n\nThe GPS interface is the cleanest we tested. Distances are large, high-contrast, and readable in direct sunlight. The hole flyover shows hazard distances and layup targets. The Apple Watch app mirrors the key data without trying to cram the full phone experience onto a small screen.\n\nThe premium tier ($40/year) adds a virtual caddie that recommends clubs based on your history and conditions. After 10+ rounds of data, the suggestions were surprisingly accurate — it learned that we club up in wind faster than we do manually. Skip this feature until you have logged at least 10 rounds.\n\nThe weakness: 18Birdies tries to do everything, which means nothing feels as deep as a specialist app. The shot tracking is manual (tap after each shot), which most golfers abandon by hole 6. If automatic shot tracking is your priority, Arccos is better.",
