@@ -4160,15 +4160,15 @@ export const ARTICLES: Article[] = [
     toc: ['Why most improvement approaches are wrong', 'The biggest source of wasted strokes', 'Short game improvement (biggest ROI)', 'Course management changes that work immediately', 'Equipment changes that help vs hurt', 'Tracking your progress', 'FAQ'],
     sections: [
       { h2: 'Why Most Improvement Plans Fail', body: 'The average golfer practices driver 60 percent of the time, irons 30 percent, and short game 10 percent. The math on where you actually lose strokes is the exact opposite: 65 percent of your strokes happen inside 100 yards, but you spend 90 percent of practice time on shots outside 100 yards. Flipping that ratio — spending at least half your practice on putting and chipping — is the single fastest way to lower your handicap without changing your swing. This is not opinion. I tracked my practice split for 6 months, then reversed it. My handicap dropped from 16 to 12 in that period without a single swing lesson.' },
-      { h2: 'The Biggest Source of Wasted Strokes: Disaster Holes', body: 'A 20-handicapper who shoots 92 does not make 20 bogeys. They make 12 bogeys, 2 pars, and 4 disaster holes (double bogey or worse). Those 4 disasters add 8+ extra strokes. Eliminate two of them and you shoot 88 — a 16 handicap — without hitting a single better golf shot. How to cut disasters: never follow a bad shot with a hero shot. After an OB drive, lay up to your favorite yardage instead of trying to reach the green from the trees. After a chunked chip, putt from the fringe instead of trying another chip. The goal is damage control. A bogey after trouble is a win. A double after a bad drive means the situation got worse, not better. Track your disasters per round for 5 rounds — you will find they cluster on the same 3 to 4 holes. Those holes need a conservative strategy, not a better swing.' },
+      { h2: 'The Biggest Source of Wasted Strokes: Disaster Holes', body: 'A 20-handicapper who shoots 92 does not make 20 bogeys. They make 12 bogeys, 2 pars, and 4 disaster holes (double bogey or worse). Those 4 disasters add 8+ extra strokes. Eliminate two of them and you shoot 88 — a 16 handicap — without hitting a single better golf shot. How to cut disasters: never follow a bad shot with a hero shot. After an OB drive, lay up to your favorite yardage instead of trying to reach the green from the trees. After a chunked chip, putt from the fringe instead of trying another chip. The goal is damage control. A bogey after trouble is a win. A double after a bad drive means the situation got worse, not better. Track your disasters per round over multiple rounds — you will find they cluster on the same 3 to 4 holes. Those holes need a conservative strategy, not a better swing.' },
       { h2: 'Short Game Has the Highest ROI', body: 'Putting and chipping improvements translate to scores faster than any other skill because they happen more often. You putt on every hole (36 putts per round for a 20-handicapper) and chip on at least 10 holes. Reducing your average putts from 36 to 33 saves 3 strokes per round — that alone drops your handicap by 2 points. Getting up and down from around the green just one more time per round saves another stroke. Combined, that is 4 strokes from short game alone without touching a driver or iron. Start with <a href="/how-to-stop-3-putting/">eliminating three-putts</a> — that is the single highest-leverage putting fix. Then work on basic <a href="/how-to-chip-in-golf/">chipping technique</a> to get the ball on the green consistently from 20 yards and in.' },
       { h2: 'Course Management: Play Smarter, Not Better', body: 'Course management is free strokes. Play to your miss: if you slice, aim at the left side of the fairway and let the slice bring it back to center. If you hit a draw, aim right. On approach shots, always aim at the fat part of the green — never at a pin tucked behind a bunker, even if you feel confident. The math: aiming center-green from 150 yards gives you a 30-foot putt. Aiming at a tight pin gives you either a 10-footer (20 percent chance) or a bunker shot (40 percent chance) or a 40-footer from the wrong side (40 percent chance). The expected score from center-green aim is lower every time. Lay up short of trouble on par 5s instead of going for it in two. Two easy shots to the green beat one hero shot into the water. For a full scoring strategy, see our <a href="/how-to-break-90/">how to break 90 roadmap</a>.' },
       { h2: 'How to Structure Your Practice Week', body: 'Here is the weekly practice split that produces the fastest handicap drop for a golfer who practices 2 to 3 hours per week. Session 1 (45 minutes): putting only. Spend 15 minutes on 3-foot makes (gate drill), 15 minutes on lag putting from 30 feet (towel drill), and 15 minutes on 6-foot putts (clock drill). Session 2 (45 minutes): chipping and pitching. Spend 20 minutes on basic chips from 10 to 20 yards, 15 minutes on bunker shots, and 10 minutes on 50-yard pitches. Session 3 (30 minutes): full swing at the range — but with purpose. Hit every shot at a specific target. Alternate clubs every 3 balls. Finish with 10 driver swings, not 50. This ratio — 50 percent short game, 30 percent chipping, 20 percent full swing — mirrors where strokes are actually lost on the course.' },
       { h2: 'Handicap Milestones: 20 to 15 to 10', body: 'Each milestone requires a different focus. From 20 to 15: eliminate disaster holes and three-putts. Stop hitting driver on every hole. Play the safe shot. This is almost entirely course management — no swing changes needed. Most golfers can reach 15 within 6 months of deliberate play. From 15 to 10: improve ball striking consistency. Take lessons to clean up your impact position. Learn to control trajectory and shape. This takes 12 to 18 months and usually requires professional instruction. From 10 to 5: refine distance control with wedges, become a reliable putter from 6 to 10 feet, and develop a dependable pre-shot routine. This is the hardest drop because each stroke gained requires more precision. See where your handicap sits relative to the <a href="/average-golf-handicap/">average by age group</a> — knowing the baseline helps set realistic expectations.' },
       { h2: 'The Mental Game and Pre-Shot Routine', body: 'Blow-up holes are 70 percent mental. After a bad shot, your brain floods with frustration, and the next shot is a revenge attempt — a forced hero play that makes things worse. A simple pre-shot routine breaks this cycle. Step 1: pick your target (the safe, smart target, not the hero target). Step 2: take one practice swing visualizing the shot shape you want. Step 3: address the ball and go within 10 seconds. Do not stand over the ball thinking. The routine takes 15 seconds and prevents the emotional cascade that turns a bogey into a triple. Every shot gets the same routine — the great ones and the recovery shots. Consistency breeds confidence, and confidence prevents blow-ups. I used to average 2.5 triples per round before adopting a pre-shot routine. After 3 months of using the same routine on every shot, my triples dropped to 0.8 per round — a 5-stroke improvement from process alone, no swing changes.' },
-      { h2: 'Track the Right Stats', body: 'Most golfers track total score and nothing else. To lower your handicap, track these five stats for 5 rounds: fairways hit (out of 14), greens in regulation (out of 18), total putts, up-and-down percentage (chips/pitches that save par), and penalty strokes. After 5 rounds, calculate your averages. Your worst stat is your biggest opportunity. Fairways under 5 per round: your tee shots are costing you — consider hitting 3-wood or hybrid more often. Greens under 5: your approach irons need work. Putts over 36: putting is the priority. Up-and-down under 20 percent: chipping is the priority. Penalties over 2 per round: course management is the priority. Use our <a href="/golf-handicap-calculator/">handicap calculator</a> to track your progress as you apply these fixes.' },
+      { h2: 'Track the Right Stats', body: 'Most golfers track total score and nothing else. To lower your handicap, track these five stats over multiple rounds: fairways hit (out of 14), greens in regulation (out of 18), total putts, up-and-down percentage (chips/pitches that save par), and penalty strokes. After a full season, calculate your averages. Your worst stat is your biggest opportunity. Fairways under 5 per round: your tee shots are costing you — consider hitting 3-wood or hybrid more often. Greens under 5: your approach irons need work. Putts over 36: putting is the priority. Up-and-down under 20 percent: chipping is the priority. Penalties over 2 per round: course management is the priority. Use our <a href="/golf-handicap-calculator/">handicap calculator</a> to track your progress as you apply these fixes.' },
       { h2: 'Equipment Changes That Actually Help', body: 'Most equipment changes do not lower your handicap. A new driver adds 5 to 10 yards but does not fix a slice. New irons feel better but do not change your impact position. The equipment changes that DO help: getting fitted for proper shaft flex (a shaft that is too stiff costs 15 to 20 yards), switching to game-improvement irons if you play blades above a 15 handicap, and matching your golf ball compression to your swing speed. The biggest equipment ROI under $100: a quality putting mirror ($25), alignment sticks ($12), and a dozen compression-matched balls ($25 to $35). These three purchases improve your practice quality more than a $500 driver ever will.' },
-      { h2: 'The 5-Round Data Collection Plan', body: 'For the next 5 rounds, carry a simple scorecard with extra columns. After each hole, write down: fairway hit (Y/N), green in regulation (Y/N), number of putts, whether you got up and down from off the green (Y/N), and any penalty strokes. After 5 rounds, average each column. You now have a data-driven picture of where your strokes go. The number one mistake golfers make is practicing their strengths instead of their weaknesses. The data tells you exactly what to practice. One of our testers discovered he was averaging 38 putts per round but spending 80 percent of practice time on driver. He flipped the ratio, and his handicap dropped 3 points in 8 weeks without changing his swing. Data beats guesswork every time.' },
+      { h2: 'The 5-Round Data Collection Plan', body: 'For the next 5 rounds, carry a simple scorecard with extra columns. After each hole, write down: fairway hit (Y/N), green in regulation (Y/N), number of putts, whether you got up and down from off the green (Y/N), and any penalty strokes. After a full season, average each column. You now have a data-driven picture of where your strokes go. The number one mistake golfers make is practicing their strengths instead of their weaknesses. The data tells you exactly what to practice. One of our testers discovered he was averaging 38 putts per round but spending 80 percent of practice time on driver. He flipped the ratio, and his handicap dropped 3 points in 8 weeks without changing his swing. Data beats guesswork every time.' },
     ],
     faq: [
       { q: 'How long does it take to lower your handicap by 5 strokes?', a: 'With focused practice 2 to 3 times per week and 1 round per week, most golfers drop 5 strokes in 4 to 6 months. The first 3 strokes come fast from eliminating disasters and three-putts. The next 2 require more deliberate short-game work and often a lesson or two.' },
