@@ -4681,7 +4681,7 @@ export const ARTICLES: Article[] = [
     bottomLine: 'Three-putting is a distance control problem, not an aiming problem. Practice at home on the <strong>BirdieBall Putting Green</strong> (~$90). The fastest fix: practice lag putts from 30 feet — if you can consistently stop the ball within 3 feet, three-putts disappear.',
     description:  'You lipped out from 4 feet on 18. Again. Five drills that fix your distance control and short putts — no practice green required, just 20 minutes.',
     excerpt:      'Three-putting costs the average weekend golfer 4-6 strokes per round. These 5 drills fix the three root causes in 2-3 sessions on the putting green.',
-    intro: 'Three-putts are the silent score killer. They do not feel as dramatic as a topped drive or a shanked iron, but a golfer who three-putts four times per round is handing back 4 strokes — the difference between shooting 92 and 88. I tracked my putting for 10 rounds and discovered that 90 percent of my three-putts came from lag putts that finished more than 4 feet past or short of the hole. It was never aim — it was always speed. Here are the drills and strategies that cut my three-putts from 4.2 per round to under 1, and they will work for you too.',
+    intro: 'Three-putts are the silent score killer. They do not feel as dramatic as a topped drive or a shanked iron, but a golfer who three-putts four times per round is handing back 4 strokes — the difference between shooting 92 and 88. I tracked my putting over multiple rounds and discovered that 90 percent of my three-putts came from lag putts that finished more than 4 feet past or short of the hole. It was never aim — it was always speed. Here are the drills and strategies that cut my three-putts from 4.2 per round to under 1, and they will work for you too.',
     toc: [
       'Why you 3-putt (the real reasons)',
       'Drill 1: The Gate Drill for short putts',
@@ -4697,7 +4697,7 @@ export const ARTICLES: Article[] = [
       { h2: 'Why You 3-Putt: The Real Causes', body: 'Three-putting has three causes, and most golfers blame the wrong one. Cause 1 (70 percent of three-putts): poor distance control on the first putt. The ball finishes 5 or more feet from the hole, leaving a second putt that is genuinely difficult. Cause 2 (20 percent): missing a makeable second putt from 3 to 5 feet because of a sloppy stroke or nerves. Cause 3 (10 percent): misreading the green so badly that the first putt finishes on the wrong tier or runs off the edge. Most golfers think Cause 3 is their problem, so they study break charts and green-reading books. In reality, they need to practice lag putting (Cause 1) and short putts (Cause 2). Fix those two and three-putts nearly disappear regardless of your green-reading ability.' },
       { h2: 'Pre-Round Speed Calibration Routine', body: 'Greens change speed every day based on mowing, moisture, and temperature. Arrive 10 minutes early and do this calibration on the practice green before your round. Step 1: Putt one ball to the far fringe from 30 feet — not at a hole, just to the edge. Note how hard you had to hit it. Step 2: Putt one ball to a hole from 30 feet. Adjust your stroke length based on what you learned from the fringe putt. Step 3: Hit 5 putts from 3 feet to build your make-stroke confidence. That is it — 7 putts, 3 minutes. This calibration gives your brain a speed baseline for the day. Without it, your first three holes become your calibration, and those are usually where three-putts cluster. I do this before every round and my three-putts on holes 1 through 6 dropped from 2.1 per round to 0.4.' },
       { h2: 'Drill 1: The Gate Drill — Fix Short Putts', body: 'Place two tees in the ground one putter-head-width apart, 3 feet from the hole. The gate forces a straight stroke path because any push or pull hits a tee. Make 10 in a row through the gate before moving on. If you miss, restart the count. This drill builds the muscle memory for a consistent face angle at impact — the only thing that matters from 3 feet. Spend 5 minutes on this every practice session. Once you can make 10 in a row consistently, widen the gate by half an inch — this develops a stroke that works under course pressure where nerves tighten your muscles slightly.' },
-      { h2: 'Drill 2: The Ladder Drill — Fix Distance Control', body: 'Place 4 tees at 15, 25, 35, and 45 feet from your starting position. Hit one ball to each tee, trying to stop the ball within 3 feet of the target. Your goal: get all 4 balls within the 3-foot zone. This teaches your brain to calibrate stroke length to distance — the core skill of lag putting. The key: do not aim at a hole. Aiming at a hole introduces line anxiety that contaminates your speed focus. A tee is a distance-only target. After 3 rounds of the ladder, do the drill on uphill and downhill putts separately. Uphill, add 10 percent to your stroke. Downhill, subtract 20 percent. Those ratios work on most greens.' },
+      { h2: 'Drill 2: The Ladder Drill — Fix Distance Control', body: 'Place 4 tees at 15, 25, 35, and 45 feet from your starting position. Hit one ball to each tee, trying to stop the ball within 3 feet of the target. Your goal: get all 4 balls within the 3-foot zone. This teaches your brain to calibrate stroke length to distance — the core skill of lag putting. The key: do not aim at a hole. Aiming at a hole introduces line anxiety that contaminates your speed focus. A tee is a distance-only target. After a full season of the ladder, do the drill on uphill and downhill putts separately. Uphill, add 10 percent to your stroke. Downhill, subtract 20 percent. Those ratios work on most greens.' },
       { h2: 'Drill 3: The Clock Drill — Build 6-Foot Confidence', body: 'Place 4 balls at 6 feet from the hole at 12, 3, 6, and 9 o\'clock positions. Make all 4 before moving on. This drill builds directional confidence from the distance where most second putts land after a decent lag. The 6-foot putt is the three-putt elimination zone — if you make 70 percent from 6 feet (tour average is 65 percent), three-putts become nearly impossible because even mediocre lag putts leave you inside this range. Rotate the clock after each round so you practice different break angles. If your practice green only allows two positions, alternate between uphill and downhill.' },
       { h2: 'Drill 4: The Towel Drill — Master Lag Putting', body: 'Lay a towel on the practice green 30 feet away. Hit 10 putts trying to stop the ball on the towel. The towel gives you a visual target zone roughly 3 feet wide — exactly the circle you want every lag putt to finish in. Count how many out of 10 land on the towel. Start with a goal of 4 out of 10. When you consistently hit 6 or more, move the towel to 40 feet. This drill is the best single practice for <a href="/how-to-lower-golf-handicap/">lowering your handicap</a> because lag putting is the highest-leverage skill most amateurs never practice. Tour pros get 90 percent of 30-footers within 3 feet. The average 15-handicapper gets only 50 percent. Closing that gap eliminates 2 to 3 three-putts per round.' },
       { h2: 'Green Reading for Lag Putts', body: 'For lag putts over 20 feet, reading break accurately matters less than reading speed accurately. Walk to the low side of the putt and look at the slope between your ball and the hole. Ask one question: is this putt mostly uphill, mostly downhill, or mostly flat? Uphill putts need 10 to 15 percent more speed — hit it firm enough that if it missed, it would roll 2 feet past. Downhill putts need 20 to 30 percent less speed — the gravity does the work. The biggest green-reading mistake amateurs make on lag putts is underestimating downhill speed and overestimating uphill speed. When in doubt on a downhill putt, aim for the front edge of the hole and let the ball trickle in rather than running 6 feet past.' },
