     sections: [
       { h2: 'Head-to-Head Comparison Table', body: "Before diving into details, here is the quick overview. The Garmin leads in every golf-specific category. The Apple Watch leads in every smartwatch category. The question is which set of priorities matters more to you." },
       { h2: 'GPS Accuracy and Course Maps', body: "The Garmin S62 has 42,000 courses preloaded with full-color hole maps showing fairway shape, bunker positions, water hazards, and out-of-bounds lines. Green View lets you drag the pin to its actual position for precise approach yardages. Hazard distances pop up automatically as you approach them.\n\nThe Apple Watch with Golfshot shows basic hole layouts and front/middle/back yardages. The maps are functional but not as detailed — no Green View pin dragging, no automatic hazard detection. You see distances but not the visual context that helps with course management.\n\nGPS accuracy is comparable — both are within 2-3 yards of each other on middle-of-green readings. The difference is in the information surrounding that number. The Garmin tells you the distance AND shows you what is between you and the green. The Apple Watch gives you the distance and leaves the rest to your eyes.\n\nWinner: Garmin S62 — the course maps and hazard detection are a tier above anything available on Apple Watch." },
-      { h2: 'Shot Tracking and Performance Data', body: "The Garmin S62 tracks shots using GPS position changes. You can manually tag clubs after each shot or let the watch auto-detect based on distance patterns. After 15 rounds, auto-detection was correct about 70% of the time. Post-round, shots sync to the Garmin Golf app with a full hole-by-hole shot map.\n\nThe Apple Watch with Golfshot also tracks shots via GPS, but requires manual club input on every shot. The Arccos app on Apple Watch provides automatic tracking if you buy Arccos sensors ($179 + $99/year subscription). Without Arccos, Apple Watch shot tracking is entirely manual.\n\nThe Garmin Golf app provides club averages, scoring trends, and basic strokes-gained data — all free, no subscription. Golfshot premium costs $30/year for full stat tracking. Arccos costs $99/year on top of sensor hardware.\n\nWinner: Garmin S62 — better built-in tracking with no subscription required. Apple Watch wins only if you add Arccos sensors, which adds $179 + $99/year to the cost." },
