         <p style="margin:0 0 14px;">Golf accessories are the most overlooked part of equipment shopping. People will spend $500 on a new driver to gain three yards, then refuse to spend $25 on a glove that grips properly in the heat. The category is full of cheap upgrades that genuinely improve your round, and full of $80 gadgets that promise miracles and deliver nothing.</p>
         <p style="margin:0 0 14px;">This hub is organized by what the accessory actually does for your game. <strong>Performance accessories</strong> — gloves, push carts, <a href="/best-golf-shoes-for-walking/">walking shoes</a>, <a href="/best-golf-training-aids/">training aids</a> — change the physical experience of playing. <strong>Maintenance accessories</strong> — divot tools, towels, club brushes, ball markers — keep your gear and your manners in good shape. <strong>Gift accessories</strong> — <a href="/best-golf-gifts-for-dad/">Father's Day picks</a>, holiday gifts, <a href="/best-golf-gifts-for-couples/">gifts for golfing couples</a> — are organized by recipient because that is the actual problem you are trying to solve when shopping.</p>
